I know what you're saying.
我知道你在说什么。

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.869
And yeah, I felt that too.
是的，我也有这样的感觉。

00:27:56.869 --> 00:28:12.029
And again, for me personally, because comedy was sort of a new area for me to be working in, I felt grateful to it because I thought, you know, they're noticing that this is what I'm good at and they're gonna write towards that.
再说一次，对我个人来说，因为喜剧对我来说是一个新的工作领域，我对此感到感激，因为我想，你知道，他们注意到这就是我所擅长的，他们会为此而写。

00:28:12.029 --> 00:28:16.990
So I'm not gonna have to really like learn on the fly so much.
所以我不必那么喜欢即时学习。

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:20.910
It became much more familiar.
它变得更加熟悉了。

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:31.150
And I do get asked that a lot I feel when doing, or certainly maybe back in the day when we were doing more press about the show, it's like, how are you similar to Robin?
我确实被问到，在做的时候，或者肯定是在我们对这部剧做更多新闻报道的时候，我有很多感觉，就像，你和罗宾有什么相似之处？

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:33.309
And I'm like, I don't know, it's kind of blending right now.
我想，我不知道，现在有点混合。

00:28:33.309 --> 00:28:44.470
Like it's a hard now separating the self from the character is becoming more challenging because it's becoming so much more intimate.
现在将自我与角色分开变得越来越困难，因为它变得更加亲密。

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:46.390
We're all becoming so much more intimate.
我们都变得更加亲密。

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:55.029
And, you know, even the storylines have like started becoming, you know, and I think you've talked about that, Craig, about like we were all in this.
而且，你知道，就连故事情节也开始变得，你知道，我想你已经谈到了这一点，克雷格，就像我们都在这之中一样。

00:28:55.029 --> 00:28:56.670
And I think Courtney, I've been listening.
我想考特尼，我一直在听。

00:28:56.670 --> 00:28:59.869
I listened to Courtney's tooth, which is when I see your Courtney's voice.
我听到了考特尼的牙齿，这就是我看到你考特尼的声音的时候。

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:13.549
I mean, I was a little younger, but because I was in this very serious relationship, which started very early in my life, we were all sort of going through the same things too.
我的意思是，我年轻一点，但因为我处于这种非常认真的关系中，这种关系很早就开始了，所以我们也都经历了同样的事情。

00:29:13.549 --> 00:29:25.910
So it was not just sort of the quirks and the characteristics that the writers were sort of attaching us to, but it was also just the life stories.
所以这不仅仅是编剧们给我们带来的怪癖和特征，而且也只是生活故事。

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:27.269
It was also just so relatable.
它也非常具有相关性。

00:29:27.269 --> 00:29:28.269
Yeah.
是的。

00:29:28.269 --> 00:29:32.150
So it just felt very familiar.
所以感觉很熟悉。

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:37.470
I think that was huge for the show, because there's always a single like, why did this one work?
我认为这对这部剧来说意义重大，因为总有一个人喜欢，为什么这个能成功？

00:29:37.470 --> 00:29:50.589
There were a lot of shows during and after Friends that tried to be the New York, here's another group of people in New York dating, and it was really hard to get one of those to stick and work and be distinctive and be its own thing.
在《老友记》期间和之后有很多节目试图成为纽约，这是另一群在纽约约会的人，要让其中一个人坚持下去、工作起来、与众不同、成为自己的东西，真的很难。

00:29:50.589 --> 00:29:56.345
I like to think that some of it was the writing, but I do think that what you guys brought to it.
我喜欢认为其中一些是写作，但我确实认为这是你们带来的。

00:29:56.345 --> 00:30:06.668
In addition to being unbelievably talented, having so much heart and soul, the two of you having this immediate on-screen chemistry, both of you being so funny and authentic.
除了令人难以置信的才华、拥有如此多的心灵和灵魂之外，你们两个在银幕上也产生了这种直接的化学反应，你们都如此有趣和真实。

00:30:06.668 --> 00:30:07.973
See, it's still there.
你看，它还在那里。

00:30:07.973 --> 00:30:12.957
It's still there, or there's, of course, I get, I hear this is, this is me and Kobe.
它仍然在那里，或者说，当然，我明白，我听说这是，这是我和科比。

00:30:12.957 --> 00:30:17.763
I blow her kisses and she goes, like she always, she always worked bluer than me.
我向她飞吻，她就走了，就像往常一样，她总是比我工作得更忧郁。

00:30:17.763 --> 00:30:18.534
Kobe's older.
科比老了。

00:30:18.534 --> 00:30:19.034
Always.
总是。

00:30:24.789 --> 00:30:43.440
And now commercials, end of commercials, back to show.
现在是广告，广告结束，回到演出。

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:44.440
This is what I'm going to say.
这就是我要说的。

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:48.880
And then I have to talk to Josh about something, just to- But you'll be able to listen.
然后我必须和乔什谈谈一些事情，只是为了——但是你可以听。

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:53.460
But you will be probably reporting and everyone will hear it.
但你可能会报告，每个人都会听到。

00:30:53.460 --> 00:31:02.630
So one thing that I think was really beneficial is we weren't like a crazy hit show.
所以我认为真正有益的一件事是我们不像一场疯狂的热门节目。

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:04.750
you know, when we were on the air.
你知道，当我们在广播中的时候。

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:11.789
And I've talked to people about this who are fans now who have watched the series like seven times and like, it's such a huge show.
我和人们谈论过这个问题，他们现在已经是这部剧的粉丝了，他们已经看了七遍了，他们都觉得，这是一部如此庞大的剧集。

00:31:11.789 --> 00:31:13.670
And it was like, but when we were doing it.
就像，但是当我们这样做的时候。

00:31:13.670 --> 00:31:14.509
It wasn't.
事实并非如此。

00:31:14.509 --> 00:31:17.670
We were like, are we getting picked up for next season?
我们当时想，下赛季我们会被选中吗？

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:27.859
Like, and it was really like we were right on the bubble and that obviously brought in some anxiety and some stress.
就像，我们真的就像在泡沫中一样，这显然带来了一些焦虑和压力。

00:31:27.859 --> 00:31:32.883
But it also was wonderful because we were kind of allowed do what we wanted to do.
但这也很棒，因为我们可以做我们想做的事。

00:31:32.883 --> 00:31:36.804
And for us personally, like, I didn't feel like my life changed.
对于我们个人来说，我并没有觉得我的生活发生了改变。

00:31:36.804 --> 00:31:46.728
You know, I feel like if you're the cast of friends or something, it was like, you don't, you, everyone knows who you are, and you're in the middle of production.
你知道，我觉得如果你是朋友之类的演员，那就好像，你不，你，每个人都知道你是谁，而你正处于制作过程中。

00:31:46.728 --> 00:31:55.059
And everyone's anticipating episodes, like there, I'm sure that that would be very, a lot of pressure to work under and to create under.
每个人都在期待剧集，就像那里一样，我确信这将是非常非常大的工作和创作压力。

00:31:55.059 --> 00:32:18.940
So, I also feel so grateful to that, where it wasn't until like, season seven or eight or nine, certainly building up to this finale, people were writing about us and we were having to do more press and people were coming up and saying, Hi, I love your show and I can't wait for the episode and it wasn't in our world.
所以，我也对此感到非常感激，直到第七季、第八季或第九季，当然是为了这个结局，人们开始写关于我们的文章，我们不得不做更多的新闻报道，人们过来说，嗨，我喜欢你的节目，我等不及这一集了，它不在我们的世界里。

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:23.819
I remember, I have a story that came to my mind and then sorry, Josh, I'm going to talk about something with you too.
我记得，我想到了一个故事，然后抱歉，乔什，我也想和你谈谈一些事情。

00:32:23.819 --> 00:32:39.720
But when we were in, I think, our last season, and my husband, Taryn, was doing SNL in New York, and our apartment was two blocks from McLarens in New York.
但我想，当我们进入最后一季时，我的丈夫塔林（Taryn）正在纽约做《周六夜现场》（SNL），而我们的公寓距离纽约的迈凯伦只有两个街区。

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:41.559
It was on, like, 50.
大概是 50 点。

00:32:41.559 --> 00:32:42.559
And midtown.
还有市中心。

00:32:42.559 --> 00:32:43.559
Yeah, in the fifties.
是的，五十年代。

00:32:43.559 --> 00:32:44.559
Yeah.
是的。

00:32:44.559 --> 00:32:49.079
And, you know, I would fly out whenever I could and, you know, see my husband.
而且，你知道，只要有可能，我就会飞出去，看看我的丈夫。

00:32:49.079 --> 00:32:55.319
And it was wild during that time, because I'd be walking down this, you know, going to get a coffee or something.
那段时间很疯狂，因为我会沿着这条路走，你知道，去喝杯咖啡什么的。

00:32:55.319 --> 00:32:56.680
It's my neighborhood.
这是我的邻居。

00:32:56.680 --> 00:33:05.400
And people would be like coming from the bar and they'd be like, oh, they would have a total freak out because it's just me walking on the street.
人们就像从酒吧出来一样，他们会说，哦，他们会完全惊慌失措，因为只有我走在街上。

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:10.680
They're like, it's all real and it's mostly people from out of the country, right?
他们说，这都是真的，而且大部分都是来自国外的人，对吧？

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:13.920
And they would just absolutely freak out.
他们绝对会吓坏的。

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:20.920
And it was, yeah, they went, oh, this is like, there is, there is like a tourist attraction and happening over here.
是的，他们去了，哦，这就像，那里，就像一个旅游景点，发生在这里。

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:23.880
That happened to me when I would walk by the Empire State Building.
当我路过帝国大厦时，这种事就发生在我身上。

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:25.160
No, I'm kidding.
不，我在开玩笑。

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:38.039
Jordana, my wife pointed out a pretty great geographic loophole that there's no way a Brooklyn resident would be hanging out on a bar on the upper West Side, or on the upper West Side that much, like, no way.
乔丹娜，我的妻子指出了一个相当大的地理漏洞，布鲁克林居民不可能在上西区的酒吧里闲逛，或者在上西区那么多，就像，不可能。

00:33:38.039 --> 00:33:39.720
But we solved it, right?
但我们已经解决了，对吧？

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:42.839
Didn't we say, Craig, that her studio was in the West 60s?
克雷格，我们不是说过她的工作室在西 60 年代吗？

00:33:42.839 --> 00:33:44.359
Her studio's in the West 60s.
她的工作室位于西方 60 年代。

00:33:44.359 --> 00:33:47.119
A lot of TV news is in the West 60s or thereabouts.
很多电视新闻都是西方 60 年代左右的内容。

00:33:47.119 --> 00:33:51.920
We decided that was the way we decided to sort of retcon like fix it now in 2025 and explain it.
我们认为这就是我们决定进行侦察的方式，比如在 2025 年修复它并解释它。

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:55.599
Not, I don't think we ever said that in the show, but that works, let's just go with it.
不，我认为我们在节目中没有说过这样的话，但那是有效的，让我们继续吧。

00:33:55.599 --> 00:33:56.680
Oh, it would be brutal.
哦，这将是残酷的。

00:33:56.680 --> 00:33:58.480
No, she'd just have to sleep in the box.
不行，她只能睡在箱子里。

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:00.799
I mean, she would have to, she's up at 3.30.
我是说，她必须这么做，她 3.30 就起床了。

00:34:00.799 --> 00:34:11.840
I did want to say one thing, and then Kobe, you and I can have her private conversation, but I totally agree with you that the first couple seasons, I think we went on Netflix, was it season four, correct?
我确实想说一件事，然后科比，你和我可以进行她的私人谈话，但我完全同意你的观点，前几季，我想我们在 Netflix 上播出，是第四季，对吗？

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:13.199
I think four or five, I forget.
我记得有四五个吧，我忘了。

00:34:13.199 --> 00:34:14.880
It would maybe between some of their, some of their.
也许是在他们中的一些人之间，他们中的一些人之间。

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:21.760
So yeah, that I remember tipped things into us getting a bigger like real time audience.
是的，我记得这让我们获得了更多的实时观众。

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:39.760
But I do think that there was something very special about those first early seasons where we were just like, we felt like an indie band or like, like we knew we had a crowd, we knew we had, we could sell out like a 500 seat, you know, venue, but we were not playing stadiums, you know.
但我确实认为，第一个早期赛季有一些非常特别的地方，我们感觉就像一支独立乐队，就像我们知道我们有一群观众，我们知道我们有，我们可以像 500 个座位一样售完，你知道，场地，但我们不是在体育场演出，你知道。

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:45.760
The difference between those early seasons and like ninth season comic-con, which was It's like kind of an insane moment.
早期几季和第九季动漫展之间的区别，就像是一个疯狂的时刻。

00:34:45.760 --> 00:35:12.239
I remember a feeling how big the show had gotten, but I think it really allowed us to make this really weird singular show and let us, comedies really need, like one thing I'm noticing as I'm watching, the heart was always there and the characters were always there, but the show keeps getting funnier and funnier because you figure out how to commodify each character in a different way.
我记得有一种感觉，这部剧已经变得有多大了，但我认为它确实让我们能够制作出这个非常奇怪的奇异剧，让我们，喜剧真正需要的，就像我在观看时注意到的一件事一样，心一直在那里，角色也一直在那里，但这部剧变得越来越有趣，因为你知道如何以不同的方式将每个角色商品化。

00:35:12.239 --> 00:35:18.179
So I feel like we were allowed in the midst of this big Hollywood production, like we were allowed to grow.
所以我觉得我们被允许参与这部好莱坞大制作，就像我们被允许成长一样。

00:35:18.179 --> 00:35:23.780
And we were also, it allowed us to not become jerks because we didn't have to, we weren't super well defended.
而且我们也，它让我们不成为混蛋，因为我们没有必要，我们没有很好的防守。

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:29.300
We were just like, we had a really great job in Hollywood and it was really, it was a kick, it was fun.
我们当时就想，我们在好莱坞有一份非常棒的工作，真的很刺激，很有趣。

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:33.579
But again, it was like, it was a season to season proposition at the beginning.
但话又说回来，这就像一开始就是一个赛季又一个赛季的提议。

00:35:33.579 --> 00:35:37.059
And we were all the same, I was gonna say this before, we were all the same age basically.
我们都是一样的，我之前就说过，我们基本上都是同龄的。

00:35:37.059 --> 00:35:43.000
Like Kobe was like the baby by like a few years, we're all in our 20s and into our early 30s and we're all kind of the same age.
就像科比就像婴儿一样，我们都在 20 岁到 30 岁出头，我们的年龄都差不多。

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:45.460
None of us had, none of us were old sitcom hacks, right?
我们都没有，我们都不是老情景喜剧黑客，对吧？

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:48.980
None of us had had all our years and all these carbonite never run at TV.
我们谁都没有经历过这么多年，所有这些碳酸盐从来没有在电视上播放过。

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:53.539
Should we never even really been on staff on like a proper like multi-camera sitcom situation?
我们是否应该真正在像多机位情景喜剧这样的情况下真正担任工作人员？

00:35:53.539 --> 00:35:55.860
We had, you were totally new to it, Kobe.
我们有过，你对它完全陌生，科比。

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:57.460
And Josh, same for you.
乔什，你也一样。

00:35:57.460 --> 00:36:00.019
You were, you had done comedy, but you hadn't been on a show like this.
你是，你演过喜剧，但你没有参加过这样的节目。

00:36:00.019 --> 00:36:01.019
I think it mattered.
我认为这很重要。

00:36:01.019 --> 00:36:03.019
I think it made it a weird little indie band.
我认为这使它成为一个奇怪的小型独立乐队。

00:36:03.019 --> 00:36:05.659
Nobody came in quite knowing how to do the job.
没有人进来完全知道如何完成这项工作。

00:36:05.659 --> 00:36:07.579
So we figured out how to do it for ourselves.
所以我们想出了如何为自己做这件事。

00:36:07.579 --> 00:36:08.820
You figured out who Robin was.
你知道罗宾是谁了。

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:10.980
We figured out how to write for you as Robin.
我们想出了如何为你写作罗宾。

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:20.139
And we all got so comfortable together, creating those characters together, and to watch how comfortable you got with comedy, and how much we knew how to write for your voice.
我们在一起都很舒服，一起创造这些角色，看看你对喜剧有多舒服，以及我们有多知道如何为你的声音写作。

00:36:20.139 --> 00:36:23.940
And you too, Josh, like, what a gift to have the time and space to do that.
乔什，你也一样，有时间和空间来做这件事是多么好的礼物啊。

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:25.300
What a gift.
多么好的礼物啊。

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:28.860
It doesn't happen now, that is often in the same way.
现在不会发生这种情况，通常也是这样。

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:30.420
It doesn't, we really got lucky.
事实并非如此，我们真的很幸运。

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:47.900
You know, we, speaking personally, it was so wonderful to have, you know, Allie on the show to have, to have Josh, to have Neil, to have Jason, who are all like, have such uniquely different comedic voices.
你知道，就我们个人而言，有艾莉、乔什、尼尔、杰森出现在节目中真是太棒了，他们都很相似，都有如此独特的不同喜剧声音。

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:52.900
And I remember there's a few times and be like, how do I name this show?
我记得有几次我会想，我该如何命名这个节目呢？

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:56.340
How do I, am I doing this right?
我该怎么办，我这样做对吗？

00:36:56.340 --> 00:37:01.340
And it was just such a wonderfully supportive group.
这是一个非常出色的支持团队。

00:37:01.579 --> 00:37:02.420
Yeah.
是的。

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:11.820
One thing, Kobe, we were laughing about with Courtney, which you probably heard us singing your praises, I didn't absorb it, you know, I hear my name and I black out, it happens all the time.
有一件事，科比，我们和考特尼一起笑，你可能听到我们歌颂你，我没有吸收它，你知道，我听到我的名字，我就昏过去了，这种事经常发生。

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:29.260
There was something so funny immediately about your owning of the newscaster voice, like you were able to really, what went into that, like did you look at local news people and kind of try to mimic that weird cadence?
当你拥有新闻播音员的声音时，立刻就有一些非常有趣的事情，就像你真的能够做到这一点，这是什么意思，比如你是否看着当地的新闻人员，并试图模仿那种奇怪的节奏？

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:31.218
I'm sure I did.
我确信我做到了。

00:37:31.218 --> 00:37:33.177
I'm sure I did.
我确信我做到了。

00:37:33.177 --> 00:37:40.360
I think it was just the energy of trying to be serious.
我认为这只是努力变得严肃的能量。

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:41.360
Right.
正确的。

00:37:41.360 --> 00:37:43.360
And I don't know vocally.
而且我不知道声音。

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:44.572
And so aspirational.
而且如此有抱负。

00:37:44.572 --> 00:37:47.360
So you're going to be Barbara Walters any day.
所以你随时都会成为芭芭拉·沃尔特斯。

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:49.360
Just so upper trajectory.
就这么上轨道。

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:50.360
Yeah.
是的。

00:37:50.360 --> 00:37:52.360
